\section{Parameters: units, ranges and defaults}{
\describe{
\item{\code{ms_order} (wire \code{--ms-order})}{int; default absent; range \code{>= 1}. Keep only scans at this MS level. Applied BEFORE offset and limit, so ms-order 2 with limit 10 is the first ten MS2 scans. Given with no value is a usage error; absent means every level.}
\item{\code{peaks}}{flag; default \code{FALSE}. Include the mz and intensity arrays. Off by default because peaks are roughly the size of the file; combine with limit, a scan filter, or out.}
\item{\code{paths} (wire \code{--paths-stdin})}{flag; default absent. Read MANY files: newline-delimited paths on stdin (blank lines ignored; a repeated path is a usage error). Exclusive with path; offset and limit are refused with it.}
\item{\code{threads}}{int; in \strong{files}; default \code{1}; range \code{>= 1, or -1 for one per core}. Files read at once. The output is byte-identical at any value (files are emitted in input order); 1 is the default because each file in flight is held whole in memory.}
\item{\code{on_error} (wire \code{--on-error})}{string; default \code{fail}; range \code{fail | skip}. fail: the first unreadable input stops the batch with its usage or correctness error, the message starting 'Input <i> (<path>)'. skip: the failure goes in that input's files[i].error and the rest are read. skip with a single path is a usage error.}
\item{\code{out}}{path; default absent. Write the long table here, one file at a time in input order, so memory holds at most threads files. A batch that stops on an error deletes its partial table. Must differ from every input.}
}
}
\section{Returned fields}{
Each field with its type, its unit, and what \code{NA} means when it is \code{NA}.

\describe{
\item{\code{file_count}}{int; in \strong{files}; never \code{NA}. Paths given.}
\item{\code{read_count}}{int; in \strong{files}; never \code{NA}. Files read.}
\item{\code{failed_count}}{int; in \strong{files}; never \code{NA}. Files not read; non-zero only under on-error skip.}
\item{\code{record_count}}{int; in \strong{scans}; never \code{NA}. Scans summed over the files read (BULK.md section 2).}
\item{\code{returned_count}}{int; in \strong{scans}; never \code{NA}. Scans returned in columns, summed over the files; 0 when written to out.}
\item{\code{row_count}}{int; in \strong{rows}; never \code{NA}. Rows in columns (more than returned_count for a long view); 0 when written to out.}
\item{\code{on_error}}{string; never \code{NA}. fail or skip, as requested. \code{--threads} is deliberately not echoed: the envelope is byte-identical at every thread count.}
\item{\code{ms_order}}{int; \code{NA} when no ms-order filter was applied. The ms-order filter every file was read with.}
\item{\code{peaks_included}}{bool; never \code{NA}. Whether mz and intensity columns are present.}
\item{\code{column_names}}{string[]; never \code{NA}. source_index, source_path, then the single-file columns.}
\item{\code{records} (wire \code{columns})}{table; \code{NA} when the table was written to out instead. One long table: rows grouped by input in input order, each file's rows in its own order, whatever \code{--threads} is.}
\item{\code{output}}{object; \code{NA} when out was not given. \{path, format: "tsv", row_count\}; written one file at a time.}
\item{\code{files}}{object[]; never \code{NA}. One per input, in input order: the per-file block (path, file_type, reader, scan_count, source, record_count, rows_not_read, retention_time_unit, caveats, column_names, absent_fields, failed_fields, excluded_fields, error). An unread input has error set and every fact it could not establish null or empty.}
}
